# Toolchain verification.
# Reproduce the end-to-end arm with:
#   nvm use 22.21.1 && cd toolchain/smoke
#   circom smoke.circom --r1cs --wasm --sym -o .
#   ../node_modules/.bin/snarkjs plonk setup smoke.r1cs ../ptau/powersOfTau28_hez_final_15.ptau smoke_plonk.zkey

INSTALLED HERE (third-party, gitignore before the Sept 4 commit)
  circomlib 2.0.5      poseidon, comparators, bitify, smt/smtverifier, eddsaposeidon, mux1 all present
  snarkjs   0.7.6      same version the D-09 spike used, so its numbers stay comparable
  ptau      2^15 (36 MB) and 2^16 (72 MB), from storage.googleapis.com/zkevm/ptau

  Why both powers. The D-09 spike shipped only powersOfTau28_hez_final_12 = 4,096
  constraints, and the KYC circuit is estimated at ~13k. Measured on the smoke
  circuit, snarkjs turns 674 r1cs constraints into 912 PLONK constraints, a 1.35x
  factor. So 13k r1cs is ~17.5k PLONK and needs 2^15; 25k r1cs is ~34k and needs
  2^16. Both are here, so the day-1 constraint gate cannot be blocked by a download.

ALREADY ON THE MACHINE
  node      16.20.2 default on PATH, but 22.21.1 / 22.23.2 / 24.15.0 all installed.
            Everything here and every spike needs `nvm use 22.21.1` first.
  circom    2.2.3 (cargo)
  forge     1.5.1-stable, fetches solc itself (verified: compiled with 0.8.30)
  hardhat   2.28.6, hoisted to asset-tokenization-studio/node_modules, runs from
            packages/ats/contracts where every spike script already lives
  gh        authenticated as 4waan
  solc      not on PATH, and not needed: hardhat and forge each fetch their own

TESTNET
  key       ~/.hedera-testnet-key derives 0xd30DE9C5aEF8079B4718B4988E8FD1D1A96F3115,
            which matches account 0.0.10298158. Verified, not assumed.
  balance   887.3 HBAR
  live      ATS bond 0.0.10306616 answers name() = "Spike Gilt 2027"

END-TO-END ARM: circomlib -> PLONK -> verify, on the shape of the real circuit
  smoke.circom is Poseidon(3) for a nullifier plus GreaterEqThan(64) for a threshold.
  compile   329 non-linear + 345 linear constraints, 3 public inputs, 2 public outputs
  setup     1.8 s against the 2^15 ptau
  prove     1.3 s
  verify    OK

  CONTROL 1  tamper a public signal (threshold 1000 -> 999)   -> "Invalid Proof". Correct.
  CONTROL 2  witness with value 500 < threshold 1000          -> proof VERIFIES, and the
             public output `passes` is 0.

  CONTROL 2 IS A DESIGN FINDING, NOT A PASS. The circuit proves the comparison was
  computed honestly; it does not prove the comparison succeeded. A verifier returning
  true therefore does NOT mean the holder is compliant. The on-chain gate has to read
  the public output and require passes == 1. This is the shape ("cannot
  distinguish false from could not check") reappearing one layer up, in our own
  circuit rather than in the generated verifier, and it binds component 7.

  Solidity verifier exports at 36,271 bytes of source. Deployed size must be checked
  against EIP-170 once the real circuit's public-input count is known; the D-09 spike
  measured PLONK's trivial verifier at 5,417 bytes deployed against a 24,576 limit.
